{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And there was a famine in the land, besides the first famine.</b> The Ramban wrote concerning this: perhaps there had been no famine in the world until the days of Abraham. And one may wonder at him, for the Rabbis of blessed memory said that ten famines came upon the world — one in the days of the first Adam, as it is said (Genesis 3:17) ארורה האדמה בעבורך, and one in the days of Lamech, as it is said (ibid.) מן האדמה אשר אררה ה׳. But the correct interpretation is that 'the first' means first to this one, not first in the world; and so the Rav himself explained, for they would remember the first famine and speak of it, because on account of that famine Abraham had been compelled to leave the land and go down to Egypt, and Isaac his son wished to follow the way of his fathers and go down to Egypt — until the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, 'Do not go down to Egypt.'" "<b>And the Rav of blessed memory wrote</b> that this passage concerning the matter of Isaac and Abimelech is entirely a hint and parallel to the exile of Babylon. For Abraham's exile to Egypt on account of the famine hinted that his children would be exiled there; and his going to the city of Gerar, to Abimelech, was not an exile, for he went of his own will, as it is written, 'ויסע משם אברהם ארצה הנגב' … 'ויגר בגרר'. But Isaac's going there — in the manner of what is written, 'וילך יצחק אל אבימלך מלך פלשתים גררה' — hints at exile, for he was exiled from his place against his will and went to another land. And behold, his exile was from his place to the land of the Philistines, which was the sojourning-place of his father; and it hints at the exile of Babylon, which is the sojourning-place of our fathers, who were in Ur of the Chaldees." "<b>And know</b> that the exile hinted at in Isaac's affair — in that they did not take his wife and they had nothing but the exile and the fear. And at first he said, 'Whoever touches this man or his wife shall surely be put to death,' and after some days he changed his mind and said to him, 'Go away from us,' and afterward they returned to him in a covenant. And so in the exile of Babylon: they were exiled there because of the ravages of famine, and while they were there, they did not enslave them or afflict them, but they were great princes in the kingdom. And afterward they said (Ezra 1:3) מי בכם מכל עמו יהי אלהיו עמו ויעל, and the officials of Beyond the River warned concerning them, and afterward the building was halted and remained idle for a time; and afterward they again gave permission for the building and warned and said (Ezra 6:10) די להוון מהקרבין ניחוחין לאלה שמיא ומצלין לחיי מלכא ובנוהי." "<b>The verse informed us</b> that when this famine came, Isaac went to Abimelech. This was that same Abimelech who had been in his father's days, who had cut a covenant with him, and he went to him — perhaps he would treat him well in the days of drought and he would not need to go down to Egypt. And this Abimelech, on account of Abraham's covenant, did not touch him or his household at all. But the men of the place asked him about his wife, by way of inquiry, and this is what is written: 'וישאלו אנשי המקום לאשתו.' Even so they did not touch her, for they were mindful of Abraham's covenant. And this is what he said to him: 'כמעט שכב אחד העם את אשתך' — that is to say, I did not touch her and guarded myself from her, but it was nearly the case that one of the men of the land would have stumbled with her, and you would have brought guilt upon us, as came about in your father's time. End."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:27.988070063Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}